    "contentHTML": "<div class=\"learn-intro\"><span class=\"learn-keyword\">Transport systems</span> move substances from where they are absorbed or produced to where they are needed. Plants use xylem and phloem; animals (including humans) use the circulatory system.</div><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Transport in Plants</h3><div class=\"learn-table-wrap\"><table class=\"learn-table\"><thead><tr><th>Tissue</th><th>Transports</th><th>Direction</th><th>Movement</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Xylem</td><td>Water and mineral salts</td><td>Root → stem → leaves (upward)</td><td>Transpiration pull (passive); dead cells</td></tr><tr><td>Phloem</td><td>Dissolved sugars (sucrose) and amino acids</td><td>Source to sink (bidirectional)</td><td>Active (translocation); living cells</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class=\"learn-p\"><strong>Transpiration:</strong> loss of water vapour from leaves mainly through stomata. Transpiration pull creates tension that draws water up the xylem.</p><p class=\"learn-p\"><strong>Factors increasing transpiration:</strong> high temperature, low humidity, high light intensity, wind.</p><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. The Human Circulatory System</h3><ul class=\"learn-list\"><li><strong>Double circulation:</strong> pulmonary (heart ↔ lungs) and systemic (heart ↔ body)</li><li><strong>Heart chambers:</strong> right atrium, right ventricle, left atrium, left ventricle</li><li><strong>Arteries:</strong> carry blood away from heart; thick walls; high pressure; oxygenated (except pulmonary artery)</li><li><strong>Veins:</strong> carry blood toward heart; thin walls; low pressure; valves prevent backflow; deoxygenated (except pulmonary vein)</li><li><strong>Capillaries:</strong> one cell thick; exchange of gases, nutrients, waste with tissues</li></ul><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Blood Components</h3><ul class=\"learn-list\"><li><strong>Red blood cells (erythrocytes):</strong> carry O₂ via haemoglobin; no nucleus; biconcave disc shape</li><li><strong>White blood cells (leucocytes):</strong> immune defence; phagocytes (engulf bacteria) and lymphocytes (antibodies)</li><li><strong>Platelets (thrombocytes):</strong> blood clotting</li><li><strong>Plasma:</strong> liquid matrix; transports dissolved nutrients, hormones, CO₂, waste</li></ul><div class=\"learn-tip-box\"><span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span><strong>Key:</strong> Xylem = water (upward, dead cells). Phloem = sugars (bidirectional, living cells). Arteries = away from heart. Veins = toward heart (valves). Capillaries = exchange.</div><div class=\"learn-tip-box\"><span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span><strong>Summary:</strong> Xylem: water+minerals, upward, transpiration pull. Phloem: sucrose, translocation. Double circulation. Arteries (high pressure), veins (valves), capillaries (exchange). RBC: haemoglobin + O₂.</div>",
