    "contentHTML": "<div class=\"learn-intro\"><span class=\"learn-keyword\">Excretion</span> is the removal of metabolic waste products from the body. <span class=\"learn-keyword\">Osmoregulation</span> is the control of water and solute concentrations in body fluids.</div><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Excretory Organs and Waste Products</h3><div class=\"learn-table-wrap\"><table class=\"learn-table\"><thead><tr><th>Organ</th><th>Waste Product Excreted</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Kidneys</td><td>Urea, excess water, excess salts, uric acid</td></tr><tr><td>Lungs</td><td>CO₂ and water vapour</td></tr><tr><td>Skin</td><td>Sweat: water, salts, small amounts of urea</td></tr><tr><td>Liver</td><td>Produces urea (deamination of excess amino acids); bile pigments</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. The Kidney and Nephron</h3><p class=\"learn-p\">Each kidney contains about 1 million nephrons. Urine formation involves three processes:</p><ul class=\"learn-list\"><li><strong>Ultrafiltration:</strong> high pressure in glomerular capillaries forces small molecules (water, glucose, urea, salts) into the Bowman's capsule. Blood cells and large proteins remain in blood.</li><li><strong>Selective reabsorption:</strong> all glucose, most water, and essential salts are reabsorbed back into blood in the proximal convoluted tubule and loop of Henlé.</li><li><strong>Tubular secretion:</strong> some waste products are actively secreted from blood into the tubule.</li></ul><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Osmoregulation</h3><ul class=\"learn-list\"><li>Controlled by ADH (antidiuretic hormone) from the pituitary gland</li><li>High blood osmolarity (dehydration) → more ADH released → kidneys reabsorb more water → concentrated urine</li><li>Low blood osmolarity (overhydration) → less ADH → dilute urine</li></ul><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Excretion in Plants</h3><p class=\"learn-p\">Plants produce CO₂ (respiration) and O₂ (photosynthesis) as waste gases, released through stomata and lenticels. Some waste products are stored in leaves that fall off (e.g., tannins, resins).</p><div class=\"learn-tip-box\"><span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span><strong>Urea Formation:</strong> Liver deaminates excess amino acids → ammonia (toxic) + organic acids. Ammonia + CO₂ → urea (less toxic) via ornithine cycle. Urea excreted by kidneys.</div><div class=\"learn-tip-box\"><span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span><strong>Summary:</strong> Excretion ≠ egestion. Kidneys: ultrafiltration → selective reabsorption → tubular secretion → urine. ADH: controls water reabsorption. Liver: deamination → urea. Sweat ≠ urine (less urea).</div>",
