    "contentHTML": "<div class=\"learn-intro\"><span class=\"learn-keyword\">Genetics</span> is the study of heredity — how traits are passed from parents to offspring. Mendel's Laws form the foundation of classical genetics.</div><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Key Terms</h3><ul class=\"learn-list\"><li><strong>Gene:</strong> unit of heredity; segment of DNA coding for a protein</li><li><strong>Allele:</strong> alternative form of a gene</li><li><strong>Dominant:</strong> allele expressed in heterozygote (capital letter, e.g., T)</li><li><strong>Recessive:</strong> allele expressed only in homozygote (lowercase, e.g., t)</li><li><strong>Genotype:</strong> genetic makeup (e.g., TT, Tt, tt)</li><li><strong>Phenotype:</strong> physical/observable expression of genotype</li><li><strong>Homozygous:</strong> both alleles the same (TT or tt)</li><li><strong>Heterozygous:</strong> two different alleles (Tt)</li><li><strong>Locus:</strong> position of a gene on a chromosome</li></ul><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Mendel's Laws</h3><ul class=\"learn-list\"><li><strong>Law of Segregation:</strong> each individual has two alleles for each trait; they separate during gamete formation — each gamete receives only one allele</li><li><strong>Law of Independent Assortment:</strong> genes on different chromosomes assort independently during meiosis (applies to non-linked genes)</li></ul><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Monohybrid Cross</h3><p class=\"learn-p\">Tall (T) × Tall (T): all Tall (TT). Tall (TT) × short (tt): all Tall (Tt) in F1. F1 × F1: 3 Tall : 1 short (3:1 ratio in F2). Tt × tt (testcross): 1 Tall : 1 short.</p><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Sex Determination</h3><p class=\"learn-p\">Humans: females XX, males XY. Sex chromosomes: X (larger, more genes) and Y (smaller). Males determine sex of offspring (provide either X or Y).</p><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">5. Sex-linked Traits</h3><p class=\"learn-p\">Genes on the X chromosome. Example: colour blindness (X^B normal, X^b colour blind). Males (XY) need only one X^b allele to be colour blind; females need X^b X^b. Females X^B X^b = carrier (unaffected).</p><div class=\"learn-tip-box\"><span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span><strong>Punnett Square:</strong> Draw a grid. Write parental gametes along top and side. Fill in to find offspring genotype and phenotype ratios.</div><div class=\"learn-tip-box\"><span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span><strong>Summary:</strong> Segregation: alleles separate in gametes. Independent assortment: genes on different chromosomes. F2 monohybrid: 3:1. Testcross: 1:1. Sex-linked: on X chromosome. Colour blindness: more common in males.</div>",
