    "contentHTML": "<div class=\"learn-intro\"><span class=\"learn-keyword\">Variation</span> refers to differences between individuals of the same species. <span class=\"learn-keyword\">Evolution</span> is the change in heritable characteristics of populations over successive generations — driven by natural selection, mutation, and genetic drift.</div><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Types of Variation</h3><ul class=\"learn-list\"><li><strong>Continuous variation:</strong> shows a range of values with no distinct categories; polygenic traits; e.g., height, skin colour, body mass. Normal distribution curve.</li><li><strong>Discontinuous variation:</strong> distinct categories with no intermediates; controlled by one or few genes; e.g., blood group (A/B/AB/O), tongue rolling, attached/unattached earlobes.</li></ul><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Causes of Variation</h3><ul class=\"learn-list\"><li><strong>Genetic causes:</strong> mutation, meiosis (crossing over, independent assortment), sexual reproduction, genetic recombination</li><li><strong>Environmental causes:</strong> nutrition, climate, exercise, disease, education (affect phenotype but not genotype)</li></ul><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Darwin's Theory of Natural Selection</h3><ul class=\"learn-list\"><li>Populations produce more offspring than can survive (overproduction)</li><li>Variation exists within the population (heritable differences)</li><li>Competition for limited resources (struggle for existence)</li><li>Individuals with advantageous traits survive and reproduce more (survival of the fittest)</li><li>Advantageous traits are inherited by offspring → frequency increases in population over generations</li></ul><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Evidence for Evolution</h3><ul class=\"learn-list\"><li>Fossil record (shows progression of life forms)</li><li>Comparative anatomy (homologous and analogous structures)</li><li>Comparative embryology (embryos of different vertebrates are similar)</li><li>Molecular biology (similar DNA/proteins in related species)</li><li>Artificial selection (breeding programs show rapid change)</li></ul><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">5. Lamarck vs Darwin</h3><p class=\"learn-p\">Lamarck: acquired characteristics can be inherited (now disproven). Darwin: natural selection acts on existing heritable variation (accepted theory). Neo-Darwinism adds genetics to Darwin's theory.</p><div class=\"learn-tip-box\"><span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span><strong>Natural Selection Steps:</strong> Overproduction → Variation → Competition → Survival of fittest → Inheritance → Population change over time.</div><div class=\"learn-tip-box\"><span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span><strong>Summary:</strong> Continuous variation (height, polygenic, normal curve). Discontinuous (blood group, one gene). Natural selection: variation + competition + survival of fittest → evolution. Mutation = raw material for evolution.</div>",
