    "contentHTML": "<div class=\"learn-intro\"><span class=\"learn-keyword\">Ecology</span> is the scientific study of the relationships between organisms and their environment. It includes the study of populations, communities, ecosystems, and the flow of energy and matter.</div><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Ecological Terms</h3><ul class=\"learn-list\"><li><strong>Habitat:</strong> the place where an organism lives</li><li><strong>Niche:</strong> the functional role of an organism in its ecosystem (what it eats, what eats it, how it interacts)</li><li><strong>Population:</strong> all individuals of one species in an area at the same time</li><li><strong>Community:</strong> all populations of different species in an area</li><li><strong>Ecosystem:</strong> community + abiotic (non-living) environment</li><li><strong>Biome:</strong> large-scale ecosystem characterised by climate and vegetation type</li></ul><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Food Chains and Webs</h3><ul class=\"learn-list\"><li>Producer → Primary consumer → Secondary consumer → Tertiary consumer</li><li>Arrows show direction of energy flow</li><li>Trophic levels: producers (level 1), herbivores (level 2), carnivores (level 3+)</li><li>Energy is lost at each trophic level (~90% as heat); only ~10% passed on</li></ul><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Nutrient Cycles</h3><ul class=\"learn-list\"><li><strong>Carbon cycle:</strong> photosynthesis (CO₂ → organic carbon), respiration/decomposition/combustion (organic carbon → CO₂)</li><li><strong>Nitrogen cycle:</strong> N₂ fixation (Rhizobium), nitrification (Nitrosomonas, Nitrobacter), denitrification (Pseudomonas), ammonification (decomposers)</li></ul><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Population Ecology</h3><p class=\"learn-p\">Population growth: J-shaped (exponential) in unlimited resources; S-shaped (logistic) when resources are limited (reaches carrying capacity K). Limiting factors: predation, disease, food shortage, competition.</p><div class=\"learn-tip-box\"><span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span><strong>Energy Flow:</strong> ~10% efficiency between trophic levels. Shorter food chain = more energy available. Producers always at base.</div><div class=\"learn-tip-box\"><span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span><strong>Summary:</strong> Ecosystem = community + abiotic environment. Food chain: producer→consumer. 10% energy passed up. Carbon cycle: photosynthesis↔respiration. Nitrogen cycle: fixation→nitrification→plant uptake→denitrification.</div>",
