    "contentHTML": "<div class=\"learn-intro\"><span class=\"learn-keyword\">Adaptation</span> is the process by which organisms develop features that improve their survival and reproduction in their particular environment. Adaptations can be structural (body form), physiological (body functions), or behavioural (actions).</div><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Types of Adaptation</h3><ul class=\"learn-list\"><li><strong>Structural (morphological):</strong> physical features of the body. Examples: thick fur in Arctic animals, CAM metabolism in desert succulents, streamlined body in fish.</li><li><strong>Physiological:</strong> internal body processes. Examples: desert mammals produce concentrated urine, camels store metabolic water from fat, deep-sea fish tolerate high pressure.</li><li><strong>Behavioural:</strong> actions or activities. Examples: hibernation, migration, camouflage, nocturnal activity, seasonal breeding.</li></ul><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Adaptations to Different Environments</h3><div class=\"learn-table-wrap\"><table class=\"learn-table\"><thead><tr><th>Environment</th><th>Challenge</th><th>Adaptation</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Desert</td><td>Heat, water scarcity</td><td>Succulent plants, CAM photosynthesis, nocturnal animals, concentrated urine</td></tr><tr><td>Arctic/cold</td><td>Extreme cold</td><td>Thick fur/blubber, hibernation, countercurrent heat exchange, small surface area</td></tr><tr><td>Aquatic</td><td>Water breathing, movement</td><td>Gills, streamlined body, lateral line, swim bladder</td></tr><tr><td>Rainforest</td><td>Low light at forest floor</td><td>Large thin leaves for maximum light absorption, buttress roots</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Camouflage and Mimicry</h3><ul class=\"learn-list\"><li><strong>Camouflage:</strong> blend in with surroundings to avoid detection (prey avoids predator, predator avoids prey detection)</li><li><strong>Mimicry:</strong> resemble another species to gain protection. Batesian mimicry (harmless species mimics harmful one). Müllerian mimicry (two harmful species resemble each other).</li></ul><div class=\"learn-tip-box\"><span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span><strong>Adaptation vs Evolution:</strong> Adaptation is the outcome of evolution by natural selection over many generations. Individual organisms do NOT adapt during their lifetime (that would be Lamarckism).</div><div class=\"learn-tip-box\"><span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span><strong>Summary:</strong> Structural (thick fur), physiological (concentrated urine), behavioural (hibernation). Desert: water conservation, CAM plants. Arctic: insulation, countercurrent exchange. Aquatic: streamlined body, gills.</div>",
